From 894957f201c0cf44a7d285cbe563a7fb8175a68b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 15:19:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Input: ftm4 - stop sensing the way the vendor does

ftm4_stop() sent SENSEOFF and nothing else. Downstream's fts_stop_device()
sends more than that, on both of its branches:

  fts_interrupt_set(info, INT_DISABLE);   /* B6 00 2C 08 -- register 0x2c */
  fts_irq_enable(info, false);
  synchronize_irq(info->irq);
  fts_command(info, FLUSHBUFFER);         /* 0xa1 */
  fts_command(info, SENSEOFF);            /* 0x92, lowpower branch */
  fts_command(info, FLUSHBUFFER);

Our register and value defines are already the vendor's bytes -- 0x2c with
0x48/0x08 -- and ftm4_start() already ends in FTM4_INTERRUPT_ENABLE, so only
the stop side was missing.

It matters because the interrupt is level triggered and the controller holds
it asserted while its event FIFO is non-empty. Sending SENSEOFF alone leaves
the part with events queued and its own interrupt un-muted, so whoever takes
the controller next inherits a line that is already asserted. That is most
pointed for ftm4.handover, where the next owner is the SLPI reinitialising a
chip we handed it in that state.

Both callers are paired: ftm4_suspend()'s non-gesture path is undone by
ftm4_resume() -> ftm4_start(), and handover=1 by handover=0 -> ftm4_start().
The gesture path does not go through here at all -- it arms instead of
stopping -- so double-tap-from-suspend is untouched.

Verified: with this in place the touchscreen still reports normally, a
handover is still visibly taken (the ftm4 interrupt count stops moving), and
the bus comes back with sensing alive afterwards.

NOT verified, and stated plainly: this does not by itself make the SLPI report
a double tap. With the bus handed over, the qcom-smgr-double-tap subscription
accepted (id 243, 1 Hz, report_rate 65536) and an operator tapping against a
positive control, the SLPI reported nothing. That is a separate, still open
question; this patch is vendor parity, not its fix.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/ftm4.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/ftm4.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/ftm4.c
index 7f877e42176c..5b5202ed692f 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/ftm4.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/ftm4.c
@@ -418,14 +418,44 @@ static int ftm4_start(struct ftm4_ts *ts)
 	return ftm4_write_reg(ts, FTM4_REG_INTERRUPT, FTM4_INTERRUPT_ENABLE);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Stop sensing, in the vendor's order.
+ *
+ * fts_stop_device() does INT_DISABLE (a write of 0x08 to register 0x2c, which
+ * mutes the controller's own interrupt line), then FLUSHBUFFER, SENSEOFF and
+ * FLUSHBUFFER again, on both its branches. This used to send SENSEOFF alone,
+ * which leaves the part with a populated event FIFO and an un-muted INT --
+ * and because the line is level triggered and held asserted while that FIFO
+ * is non-empty, whoever takes the controller next inherits an interrupt that
+ * is already asserted. That matters most for ftm4.handover: the SLPI reinits
+ * a chip it was handed in exactly that state.
+ *
+ * ftm4_start() already mirrors the other half of the vendor sequence and ends
+ * in FTM4_INTERRUPT_ENABLE, so the mute here is always paired.
+ */
 static void ftm4_stop(struct ftm4_ts *ts)
 {
 	int error;
 
+	error = ftm4_write_reg(ts, FTM4_REG_INTERRUPT, FTM4_INTERRUPT_DISABLE);
+	if (error)
+		dev_warn(&ts->client->dev, "failed to mute the interrupt: %d\n",
+			 error);
+
+	error = ftm4_command(ts, FTM4_CMD_FLUSH_BUFFER);
+	if (error)
+		dev_warn(&ts->client->dev, "failed to flush before stopping: %d\n",
+			 error);
+
 	error = ftm4_command(ts, FTM4_CMD_SENSE_OFF);
 	if (error)
 		dev_warn(&ts->client->dev, "failed to stop sensing: %d\n",
 			 error);
+
+	error = ftm4_command(ts, FTM4_CMD_FLUSH_BUFFER);
+	if (error)
+		dev_warn(&ts->client->dev, "failed to flush after stopping: %d\n",
+			 error);
 }
 
